# Engineering Principles

## Code

* Prefer the smallest implementation that fully solves the task. Avoid speculative features, abstractions, APIs and unnecessary files.
* Prefer straightforward code over formal patterns. Architecture should reduce complexity, not maximize class count or separation.
* Check existing libraries and project APIs before implementing custom solutions.
* Evaluate existing code critically. Reuse good designs, but do not reproduce flaws just because they already exist. Fix relevant design problems without turning every task into a broad refactor.
* Prefer making code self-explanatory through clear names and simple structure over documenting every detail.

## Architecture

Design interfaces and abstractions around real requirements, not hypothetical future needs. Keep responsibilities clear where it reduces coupling, but do not split simple concepts across unnecessary classes or files.

When an existing abstraction obstructs a clean solution, improve it rather than building more complexity around it. Consider compatibility and existing consumers before changing public interfaces.

## Comments and documentation

Comments should be rare. By default, write none. Add them only to explain non-obvious decisions, constraints, invariants or workarounds that cannot be understood from the code. Never narrate ordinary methods or restate their implementation. Prefer concise comments; use longer explanations only when genuinely necessary.

Keep documentation, commit messages and change descriptions proportional to the work. Do not pad them with obvious details or restate the diff. Update existing documentation when necessary, but do not create extra Markdown files or documentation for its own sake.

## Performance and state

Avoid unnecessary work, allocations, I/O and persistent state. Prefer computing values when doing so is simpler and cheaper than storing them. Cache only when useful, and keep cached data consistent with its source.

Choose algorithms appropriate to the expected workload. Do not sacrifice readability for speculative performance gains; measure when performance is uncertain and materially important.

## Validation

Inspect the relevant code and project conventions before making changes. Test the behavior affected by the task and add regression tests where they provide meaningful coverage. Preserve useful existing tests.

Verify the result with the appropriate build, tests, linting or other available checks. Do not claim that something was tested or verified if it was not.

## Working style

Make changes directly and keep their scope focused. Do not refactor unrelated code, add unnecessary dependencies or rewrite working systems without a concrete reason.

When reporting completed work, briefly describe what changed and what was actually verified. Mention important limitations or unresolved problems without lengthy summaries.
